    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with a series of walnut cabinet builds. I'm cutting dovetail joints, but I'm getting consistent splintering at the very tips of the pins. It's messing up the fit and making the joinery look messy. Any advice on how to manage the grain or what I should check with my chisels to stop this happening?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Splintering at the tips of dovetail pins on walnut, a notoriously tricky wood, is often a sign of the grain being too aggressive for the cut. Try to keep your chisel sharp, as dull tools tear the fibers instead of slicing through them cleanly. It's also wise to check your chisel bevel angle; a slightly more acute angle can sometimes help guide the cut through the grain more smoothly, especially when you're working with a hard, figured wood like walnut. Remember, a sharp tool is your best defense against wood tearing, no matter how many times you try to force it.",
      "source": "target",
