    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i've been honing the chisels but maybe i'm missing something. i've also noticed that the splitting seems most common when the grain changes direction slightly at the end of the pin. do you have any tips for handling those specific transitions where the wood feels more brittle or unpredictable?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When you hit those grain transitions, the wood is acting like a tiny, unpredictable knot, and you need to approach it with extreme caution. It's often best to switch your technique from one aggressive push-cut to a series of very light, controlled paring strokes when you sense that change. Think of those transition points as where you need to be as delicate as a spider, not as a lumberjack hacking through a tree. If the wood feels brittle, it's often because the fibers are already stressed, and forcing it will only lead to more breakage.",
      "source": "target",
