    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "so paring strokes instead of pushing through. got it. i'll try that on the next piece. one more thing though, is there any chance the moisture content in the walnut could be causing this brittleness? i'm working in a studio that's a bit humid, so i'm wondering if the wood is just reacting weirdly to the environment.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Moisture content can absolutely play a role, especially with dense woods like walnut, which can react quite dramatically to humidity swings. If the wood is too wet or too dry, its cellular structure becomes less stable, making it prone to splitting when stressed by a sharp chisel. It's a good idea to let your walnut acclimate fully to your studio's environment before you start cutting, allowing it to reach a stable equilibrium. If the wood is fighting the atmospheric conditions, it will fight your chisel, leading to those frustrating pin tips.",
      "source": "target",
